<section class="landing-section landing-section--alt">
  <div class="landing-section__inner">
    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>Everything in the app is public United States weather data:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Radar</strong> — MRMS reflectivity mosaic (NOAA), 10-minute frames covering the last 2 hours</li>
      <li><strong>Watches and warnings</strong> — National Weather Service alerts, including the full issued text</li>
      <li><strong>Satellite</strong> — GOES East and GOES West imagery (NOAA / NESDIS)</li>
      <li><strong>Map base</strong> — Google Maps, on the radar screen only</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Requests go through a caching proxy rather than straight to the source, which is what keeps the screen from going blank when an upstream server has a bad day, and what lets the app get a frame list instead of scraping a directory page on your phone.</p>
    <p><strong>This app is not affiliated with NOAA or the National Weather Service.</strong> It is an independent viewer for imagery and alerts those agencies publish openly. For official warnings and safety guidance, follow your local NWS office and local emergency management.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="landing-section">
  <div class="landing-section__inner">
    <h2>Privacy — straight talk</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No sign-up, no login, no profile.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Location is optional.</strong> It is used to center the map and to pick the nearest satellite sector. Decline it and everything still works from the map center; nothing is uploaded or stored on a server.</li>
      <li>Network access fetches public NOAA imagery and alerts, and serves ads.</li>
      <li><strong>Ads</strong>: the app is free and supported by Google AdMob banner and interstitial ads. The AdMob SDK sends advertising ID and impression data to Google. In the EEA, UK, and Switzerland a consent form appears on first launch, and the choice can be changed later from the overflow menu.</li>
    </ul>
